Optimal Solution - Use dynamic programming to compute the minimum cost to reach the top by choosing the cheaper of one-step or two-step moves.

class Solution {
    public int minCostClimbingStairs(int[] cost) {
        int n = cost.length;
        // dp[i] = minimum cost to reach step i
        // Step n represents the "top" (beyond the last index)
        int[] dp = new int[n + 1];
        // Base cases:
        // You can start from step 0 or step 1 with zero cost
        dp[0] = 0;
        dp[1] = 0;
        // Compute minimum cost for each step
        for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
            // You can reach step i from:
            // 1) step i-1 by paying cost[i-1]
            // 2) step i-2 by paying cost[i-2]
            dp[i] = Math.min(
                cost[i - 1] + dp[i - 1],
                cost[i - 2] + dp[i - 2]
            );
        }
        // Minimum cost to reach the top
        return dp[n];
    }
}

Time Complexity :- O(N).
Space Complexity :- O(N).
